<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shikoku</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shikoku is the smallest of the four main islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, lying south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> across the Inland Sea. It was historically divided into the four provinces of Awa, Sanuki, Iyo and Tosa, and is known for the pilgrimage route of eighty-eight Buddhist temples associated with Kukai. In the 1580s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chosokabe Motochika</a> of Tosa brought almost the whole island under his control before submitting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> in 1585.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shikoku.jpg" alt="Shikoku" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Schmaltz, MODIS Land Rapid Response Team, NASA GSFC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satellite_image_of_Shikoku_in_April_2018.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
